(6245063, 921362, 50695, '<td valign="top">even though this ribboned, it doesn''t look like a "typical" posthumous image to me, which is one of the things I liked about it... but clearly dpc begs to differ.<br/> <br/>I love birds, I love captures of large numbers of birds, especially when they can contain the chaos somehow, as your does. congratulations.</td>', 'even though this ribboned, it doesn''t look like a "typical" posthumous image to me, which is one of the things I liked about it... but clearly dpc begs to differ. I love birds, I love captures of large numbers of birds, especially when they can contain the chaos somehow, as your does. congratulations.', '2010-12-05 11:41:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(6245066, 921903, 50695, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by hihosilver:</b></div><hr/><i>My only nattering naybob nitpick is actually the person''s clothes which (to my eye) undermines the classy aspect of this image.  Well, that''s my two cents...congrats on Top 10, Johanna! </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I get what you''re saying, but I feel like this image transubstantiates her clothes into feathers.</td>', 'Originally posted by hihosilver:My only nattering naybob nitpick is actually the person''s clothes which (to my eye) undermines the classy aspect of this image.  Well, that''s my two cents...congrats on Top 10, Johanna!   I get what you''re saying, but I feel like this image transubstantiates her clothes into feathers.', '2010-12-05 11:44:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(6245068, 921836, 50695, '<td valign="top">I love bird shadows, possibly better than birds.</td>', 'I love bird shadows, possibly better than birds.', '2010-12-05 11:45:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(6245083, 921580, 50695, '<td valign="top">You and Steve surprised me with how much narrative you put into your photos for this challenge, almost to the point of camp (the narrative is campy rather than the style of the photo). I like to think this is your tribute to my affection for camp and for "set up" shots like the work of <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=65886" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">silverfoxx</a> (feel free to let me keep thinking that). <br/> <br/>The "sharp blur" in this photo is just ravishing to look at, and how the face is rendered so differently than the hook.</td>', 'You and Steve surprised me with how much narrative you put into your photos for this challenge, almost to the point of camp (the narrative is campy rather than the style of the photo). I like to think this is your tribute to my affection for camp and for "set up" shots like the work of  silverfoxx (feel free to let me keep thinking that).  The "sharp blur" in this photo is just ravishing to look at, and how the face is rendered so differently than the hook.', '2010-12-05 11:53:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(6245136, 921513, 21714, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by ShutterTouch:</b></div><hr/><i> This was the fist one I looked for after the voting. It makes me ask how, what and where? As I do not know those answers; I see a space alien ship caught on an old Brownie with bad film. Such a great surge of creativity and a fine piece of art. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Will try to post a proper reply with some shots soon. <br/>Thanks.<br/> <br/>OK, I managed to upload it much sooner than I thought ... <br/> <br/>I shot it from my balcony during a downpour. My wife was away and I was home alone sick and bored. <br/> <br/>It''s been raining here almost every day now and there''s been floods everywhere. I have not been running the fans on full for a while. Doesn''t even feel like I am in Colombo. It''s a nice feeling if you don''t have to travel much since you can''t even access some of the roads when it starts. People have been carried out of their homes in boats.<br/> <br/>I focussed on the water dripping from a small canopy roof over the balcony (it was put up to fix a flooding problem during the monsoons) and shot the lamp post acrross the street. I had the camera on live view so I could see what I was doing and manually focus on the water drops. They look like lines because it''s a long exposure. What I really saw was falling drops. <br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=921524" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1301/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_921524.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1301/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_921524.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1301/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_921524.jpg"/></a> <br/> <br/>But I wanted to make them draw in the frame.<br/> <br/>The alien ship is ghosting from the lens because it''s pointing at the light on the post (bottom right). After I setup the camera on tripod and saw the ghost (which I am familiar with and have used in previous shots), I decided that it had to play an important part. <br/> <br/>It became my subject. <br/> <br/>All the other elements, well if I can call that ghost an element, became support.<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=923740" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/20000-24999/21714/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_923740.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/20000-24999/21714/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_923740.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/20000-24999/21714/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_923740.jpg"/></a><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2011-01-23 00:45:02.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by ShutterTouch: This was the fist one I looked for after the voting. It makes me ask how, what and where? As I do not know those answers; I see a space alien ship caught on an old Brownie with bad film. Such a great surge of creativity and a fine piece of art.   Will try to post a proper reply with some shots soon. Thanks. OK, I managed to upload it much sooner than I thought ...  I shot it from my balcony during a downpour. My wife was away and I was home alone sick and bored.  It''s been raining here almost every day now and there''s been floods everywhere. I have not been running the fans on full for a while. Doesn''t even feel like I am in Colombo. It''s a nice feeling if you don''t have to travel much since you can''t even access some of the roads when it starts. People have been carried out of their homes in boats. I focussed on the water dripping from a small canopy roof over the balcony (it was put up to fix a flooding problem during the monsoons) and shot the lamp post acrross the street. I had the camera on live view so I could see what I was doing and manually focus on the water drops. They look like lines because it''s a long exposure. What I really saw was falling drops.    But I wanted to make them draw in the frame. The alien ship is ghosting from the lens because it''s pointing at the light on the post (bottom right). After I setup the camera on tripod and saw the ghost (which I am familiar with and have used in previous shots), I decided that it had to play an important part.  It became my subject.  All the other elements, well if I can call that ghost an element, became support. ', '2010-12-05 12:43:11.000000', '2011-01-23 00:45:02.000000', 0),
